---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/stars/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Connecting the Dots — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Night-sky photos from four years of trips, from Death Valley to Mauna Kea, with the constellations drawn in: Orion over a Joshua tree, Mars in Taurus, the Milky Way's core, the southern constellations only Hawaii could show, an interactive globe of which sky you get from Los Angeles, the desert and Mauna Kea, and a lunar eclipse.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2025</p>
  <h1>Connecting the Dots</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Nine night skies from four years of trips, with the constellations drawn back in.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>9 photos · 7 nights · March 2022 – November 2025</strong><br />
    Dante's View · Keys View (twice) · the Angeles Crest Highway · Joshua Tree · the San Bernardino Mountains ·
    Mauna Kea (twice) · the Klamath Basin<br />
    24 constellations drawn · 2 planets, each landing on a bright dot · between 54 and 322 stars matched per photo ·
    1 sky globe of three places · 1 lunar eclipse
  </div>

  <p>
    A lot of our trips end with the camera pointed straight up. Going back through the trip posts on this page, I
    kept finding the same kind of photo at the end of the night: a dark rectangle full of small white dots, taken
    from a lookout or a garden or a roadside, with a caption saying something like "the phone's best effort." The
    photos are honest, but they don't tell you what you are looking at. A sky full of dots only becomes a sky
    full of stars you know once someone draws the lines.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I went back and drew them. Every photo below is a real phone photo, and every line connects real stars in
    that photo. The overlays are not pasted on by eye. Each photo was matched star by star against a star catalogue
    for the exact time and place it was taken (how that works is at the end). That also means the planets come out
    on their own: where the ephemeris says Mars or Jupiter should have been, the photo had better show a bright dot.
    It did, every time. The photos are in date order, and each one links back to the trip it came from.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dantes-view">March 2022: Orion over Death Valley</h2>
  <p>
    The first is from the night of March 12, 2022, at <strong>Dante's View</strong>, the overlook more than five
    thousand feet above Death Valley (the trip is in <a href="/misc/desert-2022">Death Valley, Vegas &amp; Page</a>).
    At 7:37 p.m. the phone was pointed south-southwest, about halfway up the sky. The Moon was three-quarters full
    and nearly overhead, which is why the sky is a washed-out blue rather than black. It is also why only the
    brightest stars made it into the frame. Fortunately, the brightest stars are exactly the ones that make the shapes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dantes-view.jpg'} alt="A moonlit blue night sky with constellation lines drawn over it: Orion at upper right with Betelgeuse and Rigel labeled, Canis Major with Sirius at left, and Lepus below Orion." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dante's View, 7:37 p.m., March 12, 2022, looking south-southwest. 202 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Orion</strong> is the winter sky's centerpiece, and on a March evening it is already past its highest
    point, leaning west after dark. Its two brightest stars sit at opposite corners: <strong>Betelgeuse</strong>,
    the red supergiant at the hunter's shoulder (the one that famously dimmed in 2019–2020), and
    <strong>Rigel</strong>, a blue supergiant at his foot. Follow the three stars of the belt down and to the
    left and you reach <strong>Sirius</strong> in <strong>Canis Major</strong>, the hunter's big dog. Sirius is the
    brightest star in the night sky, partly because it is genuinely luminous and partly because it is close, only
    about 8.6 light-years away. Under Orion's feet crouches <strong>Lepus</strong>, the hare he is hunting.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keys-view">September 2022: the winter sky at 3 a.m.</h2>
  <p>
    Six months later we were at <strong>Keys View</strong> in Joshua Tree at three in the morning on September 28,
    for no better reason than a sudden urge to drive to the desert (the story is in
    <a href="/misc/joshua-tree">Four Visits to the Same Desert</a>). The Moon was a thin crescent far below the
    horizon, so the sky was properly dark. These frames came out nearly black and are brightened here.
  </p>
  <p>
    The odd thing about this night is that the sky looks like winter. That is just how the stars move through the
    year: every night they rise about four minutes earlier, which adds up to two hours a month. The constellations
    that fill a December evening are already up before dawn in late September. At 3:03 a.m., looking east-southeast
    and about halfway up, Orion was climbing, with <strong>Taurus</strong> the bull above it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig tall-fig"><img src={P + 'keys-view-mars.jpg'} alt="A dark star field with constellation lines: Auriga at top, Taurus in the middle with Aldebaran and the Pleiades, Orion below with Betelgeuse and Rigel, and a ringed dot labeled Mars to the left of Taurus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Keys View, 3:03 a.m., September 28, 2022, looking east-southeast. 211 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The orange star at the bull's eye is <strong>Aldebaran</strong>. Its name comes from the Arabic for "the
    follower," because it rises after the <strong>Pleiades</strong>, the tight little cluster at the upper end of
    the line (the same cluster that is drawn on the Subaru logo). The ringed dot to the left of Taurus is not a
    star. It is <strong>Mars</strong>, which the overlay placed from the planetary ephemeris, not from the photo.
    It lands right on a bright dot that no star catalogue explains. In the autumn of 2022 Mars was in Taurus and
    getting brighter every week, on its way to opposition on December 8, when it would be opposite the Sun in our
    sky and at its brightest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twenty-six minutes later the phone turned southeast and lower, and <strong>Sirius</strong> had cleared the
    ridge. In ancient Egypt, the first appearance of Sirius in the dawn sky after months of absence marked the
    season of the Nile flood. Here it sits low in the southeast, with
    the dog's body stretching down toward the horizon, and beside it <strong>Columba</strong>, the dove, a small,
    faint constellation that stays low in the south from California.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'keys-view-sirius.jpg'} alt="A dark sky with constellation lines for Orion at top, Lepus in the middle, and Canis Major with Sirius and Columba low near the bottom." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sirius low in the southeast, 3:29 a.m. 118 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="angeles-crest">November 2022: Mars at its brightest</h2>
  <p>
    Seven weeks after that, at 1:55 on the morning of November 19, we were at a turnout on the
    <strong>Angeles Crest Highway</strong>, high in the San Gabriel Mountains above Los Angeles. It was the week of the Leonid meteor shower, which
    is most likely why we drove up. This is as close
    as dark sky gets to the city, and it isn't very dark: the glow of the basin lights up the whole frame. But the
    phone was pointed almost straight up, and up there the bright stars and one planet came through clearly.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig tall-fig"><img src={P + 'angeles-crest.jpg'} alt="A gray, light-polluted night sky with constellation lines for Auriga with Capella, Gemini at left, Taurus with Aldebaran, Orion below, and a ringed dot labeled Mars between Auriga and Taurus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Angeles Crest Highway, 1:55 a.m., November 19, 2022, looking almost straight up. 165 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Mars was still in Taurus, now nearly overhead, less than three weeks before opposition. According to the
    ephemeris it was at magnitude −1.6, about as bright as Sirius. Once again the computed position falls on the
    brightest dot in the frame. <strong>Gemini</strong>, the twins, was rising on the left, and at the top is
    <strong>Auriga</strong>, the charioteer, a lopsided pentagon anchored by <strong>Capella</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="joshua-tree">December 2024: Orion and Jupiter over a Joshua tree</h2>
  <p>
    The best photo of the set came two years later, at 1:22 a.m. on December 9, 2024, in a garden in the town of
    Joshua Tree (also in <a href="/misc/joshua-tree">Four Visits to the Same Desert</a>). A Joshua tree in the garden
    was lit gold from below, and Orion stood almost directly above it, high in the south-southwest. In
    December Orion is at its best after midnight. This photo was taken with the ultrawide lens, so it covers more
    than a hundred degrees of sky, from the mountains on the horizon up past Gemini.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig sky-toggle">
    <div class="sky-stack">
      <img class="sky-plain" src={P + 'joshua-tree-orion-plain.jpg'} alt="A Joshua tree lit golden from below against a dark blue sky full of stars, with dark mountains behind." loading="lazy" />
      <img class="sky-ov" src={P + 'joshua-tree-orion.jpg'} alt="The same photo with constellation lines: Orion directly above the tree, Canis Major with Sirius to the left, Canis Minor with Procyon, Gemini and Auriga at the top, and Taurus with Aldebaran at right, with a ringed dot labeled Jupiter just above Aldebaran." loading="lazy" />
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      <label class="sky-switch"><input type="checkbox" checked /> Show constellations</label><br />
      Joshua Tree, 1:22 a.m., December 9, 2024, looking south-southwest. 54 stars matched (an ultrawide lens, so fewer stars per degree).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The bright dot just above Aldebaran is <strong>Jupiter</strong>, and as with Mars, the overlay drew it from the
    ephemeris and it lands on the brightest dot in that part of the sky. The timing was good. Jupiter had reached
    opposition two days earlier, on December 7, so it was as close and bright as it would be all year, and up all
    night. All the winter landmarks are in the frame together: Orion's belt pointing down to Sirius and up toward
    Aldebaran, <strong>Procyon</strong> in Canis Minor (the little dog), and Gemini and Auriga overhead.
    Taken all together, it is about as much of the winter sky as one phone photo can hold.
  </p>

  <h2 id="big-bear">July 2025: summer stars through the pines</h2>
  <p>
    Summer brings a different sky. At 10:41 p.m. on July 19, 2025, outside the cabin near Running Springs in the
    San Bernardino Mountains (<a href="/misc/big-bear-2025">A Weekend Above the Clouds</a>), the phone looked up
    past the pine tops, high toward the south-southeast. Orion was gone, lost in the Sun's glare this time of year. In its
    place were the summer constellations.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'big-bear.jpg'} alt="A starry night sky framed by dark pine silhouettes, with constellation lines for Lyra with Vega and Hercules at top, Sagitta and Aquila with Altair at left, and the large figure of Ophiuchus at right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Near Running Springs, 10:41 p.m., July 19, 2025, looking high to the south-southeast. 222 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The brightest star here is <strong>Vega</strong> in <strong>Lyra</strong>, the lyre of Orpheus, almost overhead.
    Vega is about 25 light-years away, and because Earth's axis slowly wobbles, in about 12,000 years it will be
    the pole star. Lower left is <strong>Altair</strong> in <strong>Aquila</strong>, the eagle, about 17
    light-years away and spinning so fast that it is measurably flattened. Between them, and much fainter, are the
    little arrow of <strong>Sagitta</strong> and the dolphin, <strong>Delphinus</strong>. At the top is
    <strong>Hercules</strong>, and the big house-shaped figure on the right is <strong>Ophiuchus</strong>, the
    serpent bearer. The Sun passes through Ophiuchus every year even though it is not one of the twelve signs of
    the zodiac.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mauna-kea">September 2025: the middle of the galaxy, from Mauna Kea</h2>
  <p>
    The darkest sky in the collection is from the Big Island, at the <strong>Mauna Kea Visitor Information
    Station</strong>, at 9,200 feet, on September 13, 2025 (<a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Fishponds, a Crater and
    the Milky Way</a>). The Moon would not rise until late, and the clouds were below us. By 7:45 p.m. the Sun was
    about 19 degrees below the horizon, which means the sky had only just become fully dark. The Milky Way
    crossed it from one side to the other. This is the one photo where the plain version deserves a look of its own,
    so the lines can be switched off.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig tall-fig sky-toggle">
    <div class="sky-stack">
      <img class="sky-plain" src={P + 'mauna-kea-core-plain.jpg'} alt="The bright core of the Milky Way crossing a dense star field from top to bottom, with dark dust lanes running through it." loading="lazy" />
      <img class="sky-ov" src={P + 'mauna-kea-core.jpg'} alt="The same photo with constellation lines: Sagittarius on the Milky Way at left, Scorpius with Antares at lower right, Corona Australis and Ara at lower left, Scutum at top and Ophiuchus at upper right." loading="lazy" />
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      <label class="sky-switch"><input type="checkbox" checked /> Show constellations</label><br />
      Mauna Kea, 7:49 p.m., September 13, 2025, looking south-southwest. 265 stars matched.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The brightest, densest part of the band is in <strong>Sagittarius</strong>, the archer, whose brightest stars
    are better known as the Teapot. That is no accident. When you look toward Sagittarius you are looking toward
    the center of our galaxy, about 26,000 light-years away, where a black hole of about four million solar masses
    sits hidden behind the dust. (Half of the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics was for showing it is there.) The dark lanes
    in the photo are that dust. To the right is <strong>Scorpius</strong>, with the red supergiant
    <strong>Antares</strong> at its heart. The name means "rival of Mars," for its color. Below are two small
    southern constellations that never rise very high from California: <strong>Corona Australis</strong>, the
    southern crown, and <strong>Ara</strong>, the altar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four minutes earlier, the phone had turned the other way, to the northeast and high up, where the Milky Way
    runs through <strong>Cygnus</strong>, the swan, also called the Northern Cross. Its tail is
    <strong>Deneb</strong>. Together with Vega and Altair, it makes the Summer Triangle, the same Vega and Altair
    as in the mountains in July. Deneb looks like their equal, but it is far more distant than either of them and
    far more luminous: one of the most luminous stars visible to the naked eye. At the other end of the cross, at the swan's
    head, is <strong>Albireo</strong>, which a small telescope splits into a gold star and a blue one.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig tall-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-kea-cygnus.jpg'} alt="The Milky Way running down a dense star field, with constellation lines for Cygnus as a large cross in the middle with Deneb labeled, Lyra with Vega at left, and Aquila with Altair, Sagitta and Delphinus at top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cygnus flying down the Milky Way, Mauna Kea, 7:45 p.m., looking northeast and high. 246 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3 id="southern-sky">What Hawaii adds: the far southern sky</h3>
  <p>
    The same photo holds something the California photos never could. Mauna Kea is about 14 degrees of latitude
    farther south than Los Angeles, and that lifts the whole southern sky 14 degrees higher. Drawn for the bottom of
    the frame instead of the middle, the photo shows three small constellations that from home barely clear the
    horizon, if they clear it at all. <strong>Ara</strong>, the altar, never gets more than about 6 degrees up in Los
    Angeles, and the bottom of the figure never rises at all; <strong>Norma</strong>, the carpenter's square, peaks at
    about the same height, and <strong>Lupus</strong>, the wolf, at about 9. That is inside the haze and the glow of
    the city. From Mauna Kea all three reach 20 to 23 degrees, clear of the horizon. Even here Lupus did not fit
    completely: 7 of its 12 stars are in the frame, the rest were already setting in the southwest. Above them,
    Scorpius has its whole tail, which from California always drags through the murk.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig tall-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-kea-south.jpg'} alt="The Milky Way above, with constellation lines drawn low in the frame: Ara, Norma and Lupus along the bottom, Scorpius with Antares and its curling tail above them, and Telescopium and Corona Australis at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same Mauna Kea photo, 7:49 p.m., drawn for the far south: Ara, Norma and Lupus along the bottom.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="klamath">November 2025: Perseus over the Klamath Basin</h2>
  <p>
    The last photo is from just after midnight on November 28, 2025, outside Klamath Falls in southern Oregon,
    on the overnight drive north at Thanksgiving (<a href="/misc/oregon-2025">Thanksgiving, the Long Way Round</a>).
    The Klamath Basin sits more than four thousand feet up with very few lights in it, and the Moon had just set.
    The phone went almost straight up, and this frame matched more stars than any other in the set.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'klamath.jpg'} alt="A dense star field with constellation lines for Gemini with Castor and Pollux at upper left, Auriga with Capella in the middle, Perseus at right, and Taurus with Aldebaran and the Pleiades below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Klamath Basin, 12:04 a.m., November 28, 2025, looking almost straight up. 322 stars matched.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By late November the winter sky is back in the evening. In the middle is Auriga again, and
    <strong>Capella</strong>, which looks like one star but is really two yellow giant stars orbiting each other,
    too close together to split in any ordinary telescope. On the right is <strong>Perseus</strong>, the hero who
    cut off Medusa's head. One of his stars, <strong>Algol</strong>, the "demon star," marks the gorgon's eye. It
    is really a pair of stars that eclipse each other, so it fades noticeably for several hours every 2.87 days. Upper left
    are the twins of Gemini, <strong>Castor</strong> and <strong>Pollux</strong>. Pollux is the brighter of the two
    even though Castor carries the "alpha" label, and Castor, seen through a telescope, turns out to be a system of
    six stars. Below them, Taurus is back again, with Aldebaran and the Pleiades, the same bull we saw rising over
    Keys View three years earlier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="globe">Which sky you get</h2>
  <p>
    Putting all of these nights side by side, two things decide what you see: how far south you are, and how dark it
    is. The globe below shows both. Pick a place, and it shades the part of the sky that never gets more than 10
    degrees above the horizon from there, and fades the stars too faint to see.
  </p>
  <div class="globe-frame">
    <iframe src="/misc/stars/globe/" title="Interactive celestial globe showing which part of the sky, and which stars, can be seen from Los Angeles, the desert and Mauna Kea" loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>
  <p>
    From <strong>Los Angeles</strong>, the city glow leaves only the stars brighter than about magnitude 4: some 450
    of them, in 63 of the 88 constellations. Drive out to <strong>the desert</strong>, to Joshua Tree or Death
    Valley, and the range of the sky is exactly the same, since it is the same latitude, but the count jumps to
    about 7,400 stars. That is the difference between the Keys View and Dante's View photos and anything taken at
    home. <strong>Mauna Kea</strong> changes the other thing. At 19.8 degrees north instead of 34, the shaded cap
    shrinks, and twelve more constellations clear the horizon, among them Ara and Norma from the photo above, and
    Crux, the Southern Cross.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eclipse">Coda: the Moon in Earth's shadow</h2>
  <p>
    One more night, with no stars in it at all. On March 14, 2025, there was a total lunar eclipse over North America,
    the first in more than two years. During totality the Moon sits entirely inside Earth's shadow, and the only light
    that reaches it has passed through Earth's atmosphere, which filters out the blue and bends the red inward. Every
    sunrise and sunset on Earth at that moment, all at once, is what turns the Moon that dark copper color. Later,
    as it moved out of the shadow, its edge came back into direct sunlight, bright and gray again.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'eclipse-red.jpg'} alt="A copper-red full Moon in total eclipse against a black sky, its darker seas visible." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The red Moon of totality, March 14, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'eclipse-edge.jpg'} alt="The Moon partly back in sunlight: a bright gray upper edge with craters, the rest fading into Earth's shadow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coming out of the shadow.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How the lines were drawn</h2>
  <p>
    Each overlay started from the photo alone. A short program found every point of light in the frame, then used
    the photo's timestamp and location to work out where every catalogued star brighter than about magnitude 6.5
    stood in the sky at that moment. It then searched for the camera pointing, focal length and lens distortion that
    lined up the two, and kept the fit only when the result was convincing. The matched stars
    ranged from 54 in the ultrawide Joshua Tree frame to 322 over the Klamath Basin, most landing within a pixel or
    two of where the catalogue said they should be. The constellation lines were then drawn through the fitted lens,
    and the planets were added from the ephemeris without any reference to the photo. Mars and Jupiter landing on
    real bright dots is a nice independent check that the fit is right. Some of the darker frames are lightly
    brightened. Otherwise they are as the phone took them.
  </p>

  <p class="muted"><small>Star positions: HYG database v4.1 (CC BY-SA 4.0), based on the Hipparcos catalogue.
    Constellation figures: Stellarium's "modern" sky culture. Planet positions: JPL DE421 ephemeris, via Skyfield.
    Photo locations have been removed from the published images.</small></p>
  <Comments />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.sky-toggle').forEach((fig) => {
    const box = fig.querySelector('input');
    const sync = () => fig.classList.toggle('lines-off', !box.checked);
    box.addEventListener('change', sync); sync();
  });
</script>

